virglrenderer: Venus rings of one app take turns on its objects

What: one dispatch lock per Venus context. A ring takes it for each batch it reads, the
context's own stream for each command (not for the ring and reply-stream commands, which
touch no object), and QEMU's calls for a device memory's blob, a resource's removal and a
fence on a queue; nested command streams keep it. A call that waits for a ring
(vkWaitRingSeqnoMESA, vkWaitVirtqueueSeqnoMESA) or joins one (vkDestroyRingMESA) gives it
up meanwhile. vkCreateGraphicsPipelines, vkCreateComputePipelines and vkGetPipelineCacheData
give it up while the driver runs them, with the objects they name pinned (device, cache,
layout, render pass, shader modules, base pipeline, libraries); the pins go before the
answer or the ring position reaches the guest. Destroying a pinned object (or the
instance while a pipeline call runs) is refused: the app's context ends. An object id the
table already has is refused when it is added (the guest named one id twice; a pipeline
made while the lock was given up is destroyed again).
Why: each ring has its own thread and vkr let go of its table lock before a call used
what it looked up, so a ring could destroy an image, buffer or layout another ring's call
was reading (freed memory in vkr and the driver), two rings could record into one command
buffer, and QEMU exported memory a ring freed. Guest Mesa uses a second ring only for
pipeline creation and cache reads (vn_tls_get_ring), so those keep running beside the
app's main ring: Chromium's, Dawn's and Zink's compile threads do not stall a frame.
Not covered (ADR 0065): a guest call that blocks in the driver (a fence or semaphore wait
without end) holds its app's lock and stops that app's other rings; it already blocked
its own ring and the context's end.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (the spec's Threading Behavior and Object Lifetime sections: an
object must not be destroyed while another thread's command uses it).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the t- cases: a real ring stopped by
virgl-venus-test-pause.patch's hook beside the context's stream; also under libgmalloc).

